 2. How AI fixed the fear of asking for GoFundMe 
 
 By Bradley Olson 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 Photo illustration: Axios Visuals. Photo: Betty Laura Zapata / Bloomberg via Getty Images

 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Asking for money is a barrier many prospective GoFundMe users never overcome, but CEO Tim Cadogan says the giving platform is cracking the code on getting more people to take the leap.

 The secret was breaking down a fundraising campaign into a sequence of steps. GoFundMe spent nearly a year launching an AI coach that is now driving record donations. Why it matters: Deconstruction is a hidden key to AI adoption. As GoFundMe surpasses $50 billion in total funds raised, its approach to AI implementation is a blueprint. By focusing on specific pain points — like writing a headline or setting a financial goal — the company's AI tools have already been used more than 100 million times.

 The big picture: In a conversation with Axios, Cadogan discussed GoFundMe's AI strategy, the psychology of asking for money and how he uses AI as a sounding board for sensitive executive conversations.

 This conversation has been edited for length and clarity. 

 Axios: GoFundMe took 10 years to raise the first $10 billion and just six more years to raise the next $40 billion. How does AI fit into that? 

 "At its root, the whole organization is focused on helping people help each other. That basically starts with the act of asking for help. As humans, we do not like asking for help at all. Functionally it's fairly easy, but psychologically and emotionally, it is a mountain to climb." Axios: How does AI help someone figure out how to ask for money? 

 "Over the last three years, we noticed exactly where people dropped off. One drop-off was when people finished writing their story and had to come up with a title. Summarizing something painful, like an illness or cancer diagnosis, into a headline is daunting." "Through AI, we read the story, look across tens of millions of past fundraisers, and suggest three titles. Over 80% of people take or modify them, and [the fundraisers] perform better." "A second stopper was the financial goal. People wonder whether to ask for $2,000, $5,000, or $10,000. We introduced smart goals, which recommends a target based on historical patterns." Axios: What is the financial impact of using AI to coach people through creating a fundraising campaign? 

 "We originally estimated the smart fundraising coach would generate $125 million in incremental help this year, but it is actually on track to double that — more than a quarter of a billion dollars." Axios: As a CEO, how do you personally use AI to manage your day-to-day? 

 "A significant portion of my job is communication. I will draft a rough talk track, feed it my historical materials, and ask AI to evaluate and score it out of 10 on logical rigor, flow, resonance, and emotional connection to our customers." "Across GoFundMe, about 95% of our staff use AI weekly. Being disciplined and systematically documenting decisions makes it much easier for AI systems to give you leverage. Chaotic underlying processes yield chaotic AI outputs." 
 
